NUM-53: give control rules the rule step, not the routing step, when RULE_STEP > 0

With RULE_STEP > 0 the rules are evaluated once per rule step, but
evaluateControlRules() in routing.c passed the current routing step as the
evaluation's time step. That step is the width of the "=" window for
SIMULATION TIME / CLOCKTIME / TIMEOPEN / TIMECLOSED premises, which became
narrower than the spacing of the evaluations (a premise such as
"CLOCKTIME = 01:03:00" never held), and the dt of a PID action, whose
integral and derivative terms were scaled by the routing step although the
controller acts once per rule step. Pass the rule step instead.

Apply from the root of a Stormwater-Management-Model checkout at cb3e192b:
    git apply NUM-53_swmm530.patch

diff --git a/src/legacy/engine/routing.c b/src/legacy/engine/routing.c
index 9a47f4b..2866ca5 100644
--- a/src/legacy/engine/routing.c
+++ b/src/legacy/engine/routing.c
@@ -378,10 +378,11 @@ void routing_execute(int routingModel, double routingStep)
     for (j=0; j<Nobjects[LINK]; j++) link_setTargetSetting(j);
 
     // --- evaluate control rules if next evaluation time reached
+    //     (an evaluation covers one rule step when RuleStep > 0)
     if (RuleStep == 0 || fabs(NewRoutingTime - NewRuleTime) < 1.0)
     {  
         controls_evaluate(currentDate, currentDate - StartDateTime,
-            routingStep / SECperDAY);
+            (RuleStep > 0 ? RuleStep : routingStep) / SECperDAY);
     }
 
     // --- change each link's actual setting if it differs from its target
